1
00:00:00,580 --> 00:00:01,860
April fifteenth.

2
00:00:02,100 --> 00:00:04,420
Meera deposits fifteen thousand rupees at

3
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:05,100
the bank.

4
00:00:05,300 --> 00:00:06,460
Her phone buzzes.

5
00:00:06,740 --> 00:00:09,060
Your account is credited with fifteen thousand

6
00:00:09,140 --> 00:00:09,860
rupees.

7
00:00:10,706 --> 00:00:11,706
Credited!

8
00:00:11,906 --> 00:00:15,266
Credit means money in, and money in is good.

9
00:00:15,426 --> 00:00:16,066
Right?

10
00:00:17,113 --> 00:00:18,513
Khata has news.

11
00:00:18,713 --> 00:00:21,593
In Meera's own books, that same deposit

12
00:00:21,673 --> 00:00:22,713
is a debit.

13
00:00:24,116 --> 00:00:25,636
So who's right?

14
00:00:31,578 --> 00:00:33,778
First, last time's questions.

15
00:00:34,058 --> 00:00:34,778
One.

16
00:00:34,858 --> 00:00:37,178
The four thousand rupee catering advance.

17
00:00:37,258 --> 00:00:38,778
Is it April's revenue?

18
00:00:39,936 --> 00:00:40,536
No.

19
00:00:40,736 --> 00:00:43,456
The stall still owes that customer an event.

20
00:00:43,496 --> 00:00:44,656
It's a liability.

21
00:00:45,211 --> 00:00:45,771
Two.

22
00:00:46,011 --> 00:00:48,611
Paying Gopal Dairy five thousand rupees.

23
00:00:48,811 --> 00:00:50,171
Is that an expense?

24
00:00:51,322 --> 00:00:51,722
No.

25
00:00:52,122 --> 00:00:54,522
Paying Gopal just shrinks what the stall

26
00:00:54,602 --> 00:00:55,322
owes him.

27
00:00:55,893 --> 00:00:56,573
Three.

28
00:00:56,853 --> 00:00:59,653
After Infotech pays four thousand, how much

29
00:00:59,733 --> 00:01:00,933
do they still owe?

30
00:01:02,082 --> 00:01:03,282
Two thousand.

31
00:01:03,362 --> 00:01:06,242
Six thousand billed, four thousand paid.

32
00:01:07,510 --> 00:01:09,350
Now, that SMS.

33
00:01:09,830 --> 00:01:11,670
First, four myths to drop.

34
00:01:12,150 --> 00:01:13,550
Debit isn't bad.

35
00:01:13,750 --> 00:01:15,430
Credit isn't good.

36
00:01:15,910 --> 00:01:18,790
Neither one means money in, or money out.

37
00:01:18,870 --> 00:01:21,510
And neither one means plus, or minus.

38
00:01:22,147 --> 00:01:24,227
So here's what they really mean.

39
00:01:24,267 --> 00:01:27,187
In accounting, debit just means left.

40
00:01:27,907 --> 00:01:29,707
And credit just means right.

41
00:01:30,628 --> 00:01:33,268
Remember Khata's two pages, from the very

42
00:01:33,348 --> 00:01:34,228
first lesson?

43
00:01:35,068 --> 00:01:36,028
This is why.

44
00:01:37,079 --> 00:01:38,759
So which side do you write on?

45
00:01:39,239 --> 00:01:40,279
Look at the scale.

46
00:01:40,519 --> 00:01:41,839
Assets on the left.

47
00:01:42,039 --> 00:01:44,199
Liabilities and equity on the right.

48
00:01:44,599 --> 00:01:47,159
Every account has a home side: the side

49
00:01:47,239 --> 00:01:48,199
where it lives.

50
00:01:48,755 --> 00:01:50,115
And here's the whole rule.

51
00:01:50,595 --> 00:01:53,235
When an account grows, write it on its home

52
00:01:53,315 --> 00:01:53,835
side.

53
00:01:54,635 --> 00:01:57,195
When it shrinks, write it on the other side.

54
00:01:58,244 --> 00:01:59,524
April first.

55
00:01:59,604 --> 00:02:02,084
Meera puts in fifty thousand rupees.

56
00:02:02,204 --> 00:02:03,684
Which two things changed?

57
00:02:05,651 --> 00:02:06,451
Cash went up.

58
00:02:06,531 --> 00:02:08,931
An asset, home on the left.

59
00:02:09,331 --> 00:02:10,531
Debit Cash.

60
00:02:11,271 --> 00:02:12,391
Capital went up.

61
00:02:12,631 --> 00:02:14,471
Equity, home on the right.

62
00:02:14,631 --> 00:02:15,791
Credit Capital.

63
00:02:15,991 --> 00:02:18,631
Fifty thousand left, fifty thousand right.

64
00:02:19,386 --> 00:02:22,426
Now the cart, thirty-six thousand rupees.

65
00:02:22,666 --> 00:02:24,266
Which two things changed?

66
00:02:26,218 --> 00:02:27,618
Equipment grows.

67
00:02:27,818 --> 00:02:30,018
An asset, home on the left.

68
00:02:30,218 --> 00:02:31,498
Debit Equipment.

69
00:02:32,398 --> 00:02:34,038
But Cash shrinks.

70
00:02:34,238 --> 00:02:36,878
Cash lives on the left, so to shrink it,

71
00:02:36,958 --> 00:02:37,958
write on the right.

72
00:02:38,158 --> 00:02:39,518
Credit Cash.

73
00:02:41,066 --> 00:02:43,626
So debit doesn't always mean up.

74
00:02:44,646 --> 00:02:46,006
It depends on the home.

75
00:02:47,272 --> 00:02:49,512
Liabilities work the same way, from the

76
00:02:49,592 --> 00:02:50,472
other side.

77
00:02:50,552 --> 00:02:52,552
Gopal Dairy sends eight thousand rupees

78
00:02:52,632 --> 00:02:54,312
of stock, pay later.

79
00:02:54,392 --> 00:02:55,592
Stock grows.

80
00:02:55,672 --> 00:02:56,872
Debit Stock.

81
00:02:56,952 --> 00:02:57,912
And Gopal?

82
00:02:58,592 --> 00:03:00,032
Debit, or credit?

83
00:03:01,593 --> 00:03:02,313
Credit.

84
00:03:02,553 --> 00:03:05,913
What the stall owes Gopal grew, and liabilities

85
00:03:06,073 --> 00:03:07,033
live on the right.

86
00:03:08,100 --> 00:03:09,220
April fifth.

87
00:03:09,460 --> 00:03:11,460
Rent, five thousand rupees.

88
00:03:11,700 --> 00:03:12,940
Cash shrinks.

89
00:03:13,140 --> 00:03:14,140
Credit Cash.

90
00:03:14,840 --> 00:03:15,600
And Rent?

91
00:03:15,960 --> 00:03:17,160
Rent grew.

92
00:03:17,240 --> 00:03:18,840
But where is its home?

93
00:03:19,588 --> 00:03:21,268
Remember the long equation?

94
00:03:22,108 --> 00:03:24,908
Assets equal liabilities, plus capital,

95
00:03:25,028 --> 00:03:27,468
plus revenue, minus expenses.

96
00:03:28,188 --> 00:03:30,068
That minus is the clue.

97
00:03:30,148 --> 00:03:32,628
Move Expenses across the equals sign, and

98
00:03:32,668 --> 00:03:34,468
the minus becomes a plus.

99
00:03:35,024 --> 00:03:38,304
Assets, plus expenses, equal liabilities,

100
00:03:38,424 --> 00:03:40,544
plus capital, plus revenue.

101
00:03:42,124 --> 00:03:44,844
So expenses live on the left, with the assets.

102
00:03:45,084 --> 00:03:47,724
Rent grew, and the left is its home.

103
00:03:47,884 --> 00:03:49,164
Debit Rent.

104
00:03:49,824 --> 00:03:51,504
Every expense is a debit.

105
00:03:52,269 --> 00:03:55,069
Revenue never moved, so its home stays on

106
00:03:55,149 --> 00:03:56,749
the right, with capital.

107
00:03:56,989 --> 00:03:58,989
Every sale is a credit.

108
00:04:00,057 --> 00:04:00,857
Put it together.

109
00:04:01,337 --> 00:04:04,537
Debits live on the left: Expenses, Assets,

110
00:04:04,577 --> 00:04:07,177
and Drawings, the money Meera takes home.

111
00:04:07,257 --> 00:04:08,937
They behave like expenses.

112
00:04:09,486 --> 00:04:12,166
Credits live on the right: Liabilities,

113
00:04:12,366 --> 00:04:15,246
Income, that's revenue, or income, same

114
00:04:15,366 --> 00:04:16,686
thing, and Capital.

115
00:04:17,166 --> 00:04:18,686
In short: DEAD CLIC.

116
00:04:19,166 --> 00:04:20,446
You didn't memorise this.

117
00:04:20,606 --> 00:04:22,686
You read it straight off the equation.

118
00:04:23,252 --> 00:04:26,052
And in every transaction, the left total

119
00:04:26,292 --> 00:04:27,732
equals the right total.

120
00:04:28,052 --> 00:04:28,772
Always.

121
00:04:29,492 --> 00:04:31,932
That's double entry: every rupee written

122
00:04:32,012 --> 00:04:34,332
twice, once on each side.

123
00:04:34,412 --> 00:04:36,732
The same scale, in a new costume.

124
00:04:37,978 --> 00:04:39,978
So, the SMS.

125
00:04:40,538 --> 00:04:42,778
Two cameras, one deposit.

126
00:04:42,858 --> 00:04:45,418
In Meera's books, money moved from the galla

127
00:04:45,498 --> 00:04:46,458
to the bank.

128
00:04:46,538 --> 00:04:47,738
Bank grows.

129
00:04:47,778 --> 00:04:48,778
Debit Bank.

130
00:04:48,978 --> 00:04:49,978
Cash shrinks.

131
00:04:50,138 --> 00:04:51,258
Credit Cash.

132
00:04:51,822 --> 00:04:54,142
In the bank's books, the notes land in its

133
00:04:54,222 --> 00:04:54,942
vault.

134
00:04:55,022 --> 00:04:56,382
That's its debit.

135
00:04:56,462 --> 00:04:58,862
But Meera's fifteen thousand isn't the bank's

136
00:04:58,942 --> 00:04:59,422
money.

137
00:04:59,662 --> 00:05:02,062
It's money the bank owes her, any day she

138
00:05:02,142 --> 00:05:02,742
asks.

139
00:05:02,942 --> 00:05:05,222
For the bank, that's a liability.

140
00:05:05,422 --> 00:05:07,982
It grows, so the bank credits it.

141
00:05:08,540 --> 00:05:09,260
Same money.

142
00:05:09,340 --> 00:05:10,860
Opposite books.

143
00:05:11,700 --> 00:05:14,500
Your SMS is reading the bank's khata, not

144
00:05:14,580 --> 00:05:15,220
yours.

145
00:05:15,540 --> 00:05:17,300
Credit doesn't mean good.

146
00:05:17,380 --> 00:05:19,300
Debit doesn't mean bad.

147
00:05:19,380 --> 00:05:21,540
They just mean right, and left.

148
00:05:22,876 --> 00:05:23,836
Let's practise.

149
00:05:23,996 --> 00:05:26,516
Warm-up first: one account at a time.

150
00:05:26,716 --> 00:05:27,516
Which side?

151
00:05:28,436 --> 00:05:30,316
Ravi Mama's loan grows.

152
00:05:31,916 --> 00:05:32,556
Credit.

153
00:05:32,796 --> 00:05:33,796
Rent grows.

154
00:05:35,396 --> 00:05:35,996
Debit.

155
00:05:36,196 --> 00:05:36,956
A sale.

156
00:05:38,556 --> 00:05:39,036
Credit.

157
00:05:39,436 --> 00:05:40,316
Now, careful.

158
00:05:40,556 --> 00:05:43,356
Infotech pays what it owes, so the Infotech

159
00:05:43,436 --> 00:05:44,716
account shrinks.

160
00:05:46,676 --> 00:05:47,316
Credit.

161
00:05:47,556 --> 00:05:49,876
An asset shrinking goes on the right.

162
00:05:50,623 --> 00:05:51,823
Now full entries.

163
00:05:52,223 --> 00:05:53,543
Khata goes first.

164
00:05:53,823 --> 00:05:56,223
Cash sales for the first half of April:

165
00:05:56,303 --> 00:05:57,783
eighteen thousand rupees.

166
00:05:58,063 --> 00:05:59,743
Which two things changed?

167
00:06:01,714 --> 00:06:02,434
Cash grew.

168
00:06:02,514 --> 00:06:05,234
Debit Cash, eighteen thousand.

169
00:06:05,314 --> 00:06:06,354
Sales grew.

170
00:06:06,434 --> 00:06:08,994
That's revenue, so credit Sales, eighteen

171
00:06:09,074 --> 00:06:09,954
thousand.

172
00:06:10,034 --> 00:06:11,554
Left equals right.

173
00:06:12,310 --> 00:06:13,110
Meera's turn.

174
00:06:13,590 --> 00:06:16,390
Infotech's chai tab: six thousand rupees,

175
00:06:16,630 --> 00:06:17,350
pay later.

176
00:06:17,590 --> 00:06:20,310
The chai was sold, so credit Sales, six

177
00:06:20,470 --> 00:06:21,350
thousand.

178
00:06:21,430 --> 00:06:22,470
Now Infotech.

179
00:06:22,870 --> 00:06:24,150
They owe the stall.

180
00:06:24,390 --> 00:06:26,310
Meera slides it toward the right page.

181
00:06:26,710 --> 00:06:29,310
Owing money, that was Gopal's side.

182
00:06:29,510 --> 00:06:30,230
Wasn't it?

183
00:06:32,578 --> 00:06:32,978
No.

184
00:06:33,378 --> 00:06:35,218
Gopal is owed by the stall.

185
00:06:35,698 --> 00:06:37,498
Infotech owes the stall.

186
00:06:37,698 --> 00:06:39,378
An asset, and it grew.

187
00:06:39,778 --> 00:06:41,218
Home side, left.

188
00:06:41,458 --> 00:06:43,618
Debit Infotech, six thousand.

189
00:06:44,535 --> 00:06:45,855
Now, just you.

190
00:06:46,135 --> 00:06:49,095
Meera pays Gopal Dairy five thousand rupees.

191
00:06:49,335 --> 00:06:50,095
Debit what?

192
00:06:50,895 --> 00:06:51,775
Credit what?

193
00:06:54,941 --> 00:06:56,381
Debit Gopal Dairy.

194
00:06:56,861 --> 00:06:59,261
The liability shrinks, so it goes on the

195
00:06:59,461 --> 00:06:59,901
left.

196
00:07:00,141 --> 00:07:01,581
Credit Cash.

197
00:07:01,661 --> 00:07:03,501
Five thousand leaves the galla.

198
00:07:04,757 --> 00:07:05,477
So.

199
00:07:05,557 --> 00:07:08,037
Debit is left, credit is right.

200
00:07:08,157 --> 00:07:10,277
Grow an account on its home side, shrink

201
00:07:10,357 --> 00:07:11,477
it on the other.

202
00:07:11,517 --> 00:07:14,117
And the bank's SMS speaks from the bank's

203
00:07:14,357 --> 00:07:14,917
books.

204
00:07:16,126 --> 00:07:16,766
Your turn.

205
00:07:16,926 --> 00:07:17,926
Three quick ones.

206
00:07:18,126 --> 00:07:18,606
One.

207
00:07:18,846 --> 00:07:21,566
Meera buys six thousand rupees of tea, milk

208
00:07:21,646 --> 00:07:23,446
and sugar, and pays cash.

209
00:07:23,646 --> 00:07:25,646
What's debited, and what's credited?

210
00:07:26,206 --> 00:07:26,686
Two.

211
00:07:26,926 --> 00:07:29,006
Why is an expense a debit?

212
00:07:31,126 --> 00:07:31,686
Three.

213
00:07:31,926 --> 00:07:34,006
Imagine Meera pays a bill from the bank,

214
00:07:34,086 --> 00:07:35,926
and the SMS says debited.

215
00:07:36,326 --> 00:07:39,286
In Meera's books, is Bank debited, or credited?

216
00:07:39,766 --> 00:07:41,446
Answers next lesson.

217
00:07:42,867 --> 00:07:45,427
Next time, Meera's CA friend drops by the

218
00:07:45,507 --> 00:07:47,267
stall, with rules.

219
00:07:47,507 --> 00:07:48,547
Debit the receiver!

220
00:07:48,707 --> 00:07:49,787
Credit the giver!

221
00:07:49,987 --> 00:07:51,987
Is that a whole new system to learn?
